

## The key constraint
If Nginx routes directly to your downstream services, your Spring Cloud Gateway (WebMVC) app will **not see those requests**, so it **cannot** authenticate/authorize them.

To keep authentication “in Spring” while moving routing to Nginx, you need Nginx to **delegate the auth decision** to your Spring app.

## Recommended pattern: Nginx routes, Spring authorizes (via `auth_request`)
High-level flow:

* Client → **Nginx**
* Nginx does an internal subrequest → **Spring app** (auth check)
* If 2xx: Nginx proxies to the target upstream (service)
* If 401/403: Nginx blocks (or redirects to login)

### 1) Spring (your existing app): add an auth-check endpoint
You add a very small endpoint that is **protected by your existing Spring Security setup**. If the request is authenticated, return `200`. Otherwise Spring Security returns `401`.

* Example controller (Servlet/WebMVC):

    ```java
    @RestController
    public class NginxAuthController {

        @GetMapping("/_auth")
        public ResponseEntity<Void> auth(Authentication authentication) {
            // If this method is reached, Spring Security already authenticated the request
            String roles = authentication.getAuthorities().stream()
                    .map(GrantedAuthority::getAuthority)
                    .collect(Collectors.joining(","));

            return ResponseEntity.ok()
                    .header("X-Auth-User", authentication.getName())
                    .header("X-Auth-Roles", roles)
                    .build();
        }
    }
    ```

* Security rule: make sure `/_auth` is **`authenticated()`** and your login/token endpoints remain reachable. For example (conceptually):

    * `/_auth` → authenticated
    * `/login`, `/oauth2/**`, `/logout` (whatever you use) → permitAll
    * anything else in this app → usually `denyAll()` (since it’s now “auth-only”)

Also, if you’re behind Nginx and you do redirects (OAuth2 login), ensure Spring uses forwarded headers:

* `server.forward-headers-strategy=native` (common in Spring Boot 3.x deployments behind a proxy)

### 2) Nginx: call Spring for auth, then proxy to services
Core idea: protect routes with `auth_request`, and define an internal location that proxies to your Spring `/_auth`.

* Example Nginx config (simplified):

    *Spring auth upstream + protected service routing:*

    ```nginx
    upstream spring_auth {
        server 127.0.0.1:8080;   # your Spring app (auth)
    }

    upstream service_api {
        server 10.0.0.11:8081;   # your backend service
    }

    server {
        listen 443 ssl http2;
        server_name api.example.com;

        # ---- Public endpoints handled by Spring (login/callback/etc.) ----
        location /login {
            proxy_pass http://spring_auth;
            proxy_set_header Host $host;
            proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
            proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        }

        location /oauth2/ {
            proxy_pass http://spring_auth;
            proxy_set_header Host $host;
            proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
            proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        }

        # ---- Protected API routed by Nginx to your service ----
        location /api/ {
            auth_request /_auth_check;

            # pull identity from Spring auth response headers (optional)
            auth_request_set $auth_user  $upstream_http_x_auth_user;
            auth_request_set $auth_roles $upstream_http_x_auth_roles;

            # prevent client spoofing by overwriting these on the way to services
            proxy_set_header X-Auth-User  $auth_user;
            proxy_set_header X-Auth-Roles $auth_roles;

            proxy_pass http://service_api;
            proxy_set_header Host $host;
            proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
            proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;

            # if you want services to receive the original token too:
            proxy_set_header Authorization $http_authorization;
        }

        # internal auth subrequest endpoint (never exposed to clients)
        location = /_auth_check {
            internal;

            proxy_pass http://spring_auth/_auth;

            proxy_pass_request_body off;
            proxy_set_header Content-Length "";

            # forward what Spring needs to authenticate the caller
            proxy_set_header Authorization $http_authorization;
            proxy_set_header Cookie $http_cookie;

            # optional context headers
            proxy_set_header X-Original-URI $request_uri;
            proxy_set_header X-Original-Method $request_method;

            proxy_set_header Host $host;
            proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
            proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        }
    }
    ```

### 3) “Exclude routing” in the Spring Cloud Gateway app
To stop Spring from acting as the router:

* Remove/disable your Gateway route definitions (for MVC gateway this is typically whatever you configured under `spring.cloud.gateway...` or any route `@Bean` definitions).
* Cleanest: remove the Gateway starter dependency if you truly don’t need it anymore, and keep:
    * `spring-boot-starter-web`
    * `spring-boot-starter-security` (+ oauth2 client/resource server/etc. as you already have)

## Important gotchas (so this stays secure)
* **Don’t let services be reachable directly from the internet.** Put them on a private network so only Nginx can reach them; otherwise attackers can bypass your centralized auth.
* If you pass `X-Auth-User` / `X-Auth-Roles` headers, treat them as **trusted only from Nginx** (and ensure Nginx overwrites any incoming client-provided values).
* If your Spring auth uses **sessions**, and you run multiple Spring instances:
    * use sticky sessions **or** Spring Session (e.g., Redis), otherwise `/_auth` will randomly fail.
* If you use OAuth2 login, forwarded headers (`X-Forwarded-Proto`, etc.) must be correct or redirects/callback URLs will break.

This setup keeps your existing Spring authentication logic, while moving request routing to Nginx.

